= Choosing our enemies

In our media and campaign materials, who should we consider as our main opponents?

[? if enemies != 0 : Previously, we had considered the [+ enemies +] as our prior enemies. Is a change of direction necessary? ?]

- @anti_tory: The Conservative Party are our real enemy! They are less than vermin!
- @cpgb: The Communists are violent extremists and we should focus everything to opposing them!
- @liberals: The Liberals are not a party of the left, they are bourgeois rubbish!
- @easy_discard

@anti_tory
on-arrival: enemies = "anti-democratic parties"; center_strength += 5; reformist_strength += 5; left_dissent += 0; workers_cpgb -= 2; workers_tories -= 2; workers_liberals -= 2; tories_relation -= 10

Aggressively hating the tories will be good to win the hearts of voters. 

@cpgb
on-arrival: enemies = "CPGB"; reformist_strength += 5; left_dissent += 8; reformist_dissent -= 5; cpgb_relation -= 15; liberals_relation += 5; tories_relation += 5; workers_cpgb -= 4;

Attacking the CPGB is supported by all but the most left-wing socialists, and wins us some measure of approval among the bourgeois parties. They have no motion anyway.

@liberals
on-arrival: enemies = "bourgeois parties"; left_strength += 8; left_dissent -= 5; liberals_relation -= 5; tories_relation -= 5; cpgb_relation += 5; reformist_dissent += 8; workers_tories -= 3; coalition_dissent += 1

Our anti-liberal rhetoric is popular among the left, but may make it harder for the liberals to tolerate labour minority governments. Maybe win a majority first.
